        "baseRevision": null,
        "kind": "essay",
        "payload": {
          "datasetId": "word-atlas-open",
          "planId": "r0-plan-band8",
          "date": "2026-11-04",
          "day": 2,
          "taskId": "r0-task-task2",
          "contentId": "writing-sample-01",
          "contentVersion": "2",
          "prompt": "Some people believe that cities should spend more money on public transport than on new roads. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Give reasons for your answer and include relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.",
          "taskType": "task2",
          "text": "I largely agree that cities should give public transport a higher funding priority than new roads. Reliable buses and trains can move many people through a limited space, while additional road capacity may provide only temporary relief. Nevertheless, some road investment remains necessary for safety and access.\n\nThe strongest reason to favour public transport is that urban space cannot expand as quickly as demand for travel. A frequent bus service can carry passengers who would otherwise use separate cars. If it connects residential areas with workplaces and operates beyond office hours, it becomes a practical choice for a wider group of residents. For example, a worker finishing late may leave the car at home when the last bus is predictable. Investment should therefore improve frequency and reliability, rather than merely produce an impressive new station.\n\nThere is also an issue of access. People who cannot drive still need to reach schools, hospitals and jobs. Spending most of a transport budget on roads may improve journeys for motorists while doing little for these residents. Affordable fares and accessible vehicles can make public expenditure useful to more of the population. This does not mean that every route must attract identical passenger numbers; some provide an essential connection for smaller communities.\n\nHowever, prioritising public transport should not imply neglecting roads altogether. Dangerous junctions need repair, and emergency services require dependable access. In rapidly growing districts, a carefully planned road connection may also be necessary. Such projects should be assessed against clear needs instead of an assumption that more traffic always requires more lanes.\n\nOverall, cities should direct the larger share of new transport funding towards dependable public services, while maintaining roads and addressing specific safety problems. This balance recognises both the limitations of urban space and the different needs of residents.",
          "wordCount": 298,
          "parentRevisionId": null
        }
      },
      "expectedContentHash": "9b6fb2688cb2cee1a6aca37bef354d51268c08e2f31ecff9baaf4ae1af906e2e"
    },
